  "whyItWorks": "A diminished 7th arpeggio built on the 3rd of a dominant chord holds its 3rd, 5th and b7 plus the b9, so one easy shape gives you the whole dark dominant sound. The b9 sits a half step above the 5th of the minor chord that follows, so holding it builds tension the ear wants released, and the fall into {{chord 3}} releases it.",
  "fingeringNote": "Eighth position: index finger on fret 8, little finger on fret 11, one finger per fret with no stretch or shift. The triplet in bar 2 puts one note on each of three strings (little, ring, index), which suits one smooth downward sweep of the pick; the little finger then takes the b9 on the high E string.",
  "practiceTask": "Play the lick over bars 5 to 7 of the weekly standard, then over bars 13 to 15 and 17 to 19, where the same minor ii–V–i returns.",
  "variation": {
    "description": "Keep the triplets going over {{chord 1}}: the diminished arpeggio runs up, down and up again, so the b9 arrives on beat four, right before it falls into {{chord 2}}. The variation starts at the dominant bar: play the iiø bar as before, and the {{chord 2}} bar is unchanged.",
